Impacted Cerumen (Earwax)
Opthalmology & ENT
Sensation of aural fullness, Conductive hearing loss, Visible waxy mass

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-023
Reason for Visit: Sudden distressing deafness and severe ringing in the ear following a swimming session.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, complete unilateral loss of hearing, loud tinnitus, and mild dizziness after swimming. Urgent physical assessment was required to definitively differentiate benign cerumen impaction from Sudden Sensorineural Hearing Loss (an otologic emergency requiring immediate steroids) or a perforated tympanic membrane, alleviating acute distress and preventing dangerous self-instrumentation overnight.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I went for a swim, and when I got out, my left ear completely popped and went dead. Now it's just a loud ringing noise."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden, painless sensation of severe aural fullness and unilateral, conductive hearing loss in the left ear occurring 2 hours ago immediately after swimming. Endorses loud tinnitus (ringing) and autophony (unusually loud perception of their own voice). Reports mild localized pruritus deep within the external auditory canal. Denies severe otalgia, purulent discharge, true spinning vertigo, or facial weakness.
Functional Status: Independent, but highly irritated and distracted by the deafness and tinnitus.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 72 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Comfortable, frequently rubbing the tragus of the left ear.
Physical Exam: Otoscopy of the right ear is unremarkable. Otoscopy of the left ear reveals direct visualization of an obstructing, dark brown, swollen waxy mass completely occluding the external auditory canal. The tympanic membrane cannot be visualized. Tuning fork tests (512 Hz): Weber test lateralizes to the left (affected) ear. Rinne test reveals bone conduction is greater than air conduction on the left. No nystagmus. Neurological exam normal.
Environmental Observations: Cotton buds visible on the bathroom sink.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Impacted Cerumen (Earwax) - Status: Acute obstruction
Differentials:

Sudden Sensorineural Hearing Loss - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Weber lateralizing to the affected ear and Rinne indicating a conductive deficit, alongside the direct visualization of a mechanical block.

Otitis Externa - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of severe pain on manipulation of the pinna/tragus, absence of canal erythema or purulent exudate, and the painless nature of the obstruction.

Tympanic Membrane Perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Mechanism of injury (simple water exposure, no barotrauma) and direct visualization of an intact, obstructing cerumen plug prior to the TM.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute unilateral hearing loss and tinnitus, a classic presentation that mandates physical evaluation to exclude irreversible sensorineural pathology. Urgent otoscopic examination definitively identified a massive cerumen impaction, likely expanded by water absorption during swimming, entirely explaining the conductive deficit and aural fullness. By providing an immediate, definitive mechanical diagnosis, the urgent visit eliminated extreme patient anxiety regarding permanent deafness or stroke, prevented the use of dangerous cotton buds, and established a safe, conservative management plan without requiring an emergency department visit.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Cerumol (chlorobutanol/arachis oil) drops, 5 drops BD for 3 days to soften the plug.
Positive Behavioral Change: To facilitate wax expulsion and prevent impaction, the patient must discard all cotton buds in the house immediately into the outside bin. Replacing the habitual, dangerous use of cotton buds with the passive application of softening drops ensures the wax is chemically broken down rather than physically rammed against the eardrum, directly preventing future recurrent deafness (negative punishment of the bad habit).
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days for definitive ear syringing or micro-suction.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if severe, throbbing pain develops, purulent fluid begins draining from the ear, or severe spinning vertigo with vomiting occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; tympanic membrane assessment requires wax removal.
Clinical Course: Hearing will remain muffled until the wax is removed. The drops may temporarily make the blockage feel worse before it gets better.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not put anything inside your ear to dig the wax out. If you develop severe pain or start feeling the room spinning uncontrollably, seek medical attention.
PATIENT EDUCATION

